{
  "dom_id": "step-q325925",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14093765/assets/f26488cd11bddae593c3de69e04e95b4e61b35442f5a3aa8726cc8e0f8ef4600.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t.\n$$\n\nDetermine the domain of the parameter $t$ for this quarter, assuming that the entire hyperbola is defined only for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14093765/assets/cf47cf9e617f29a25ddd353448d8df1855ccf778b2f5ce304d534161f683cf16.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].\n$$",
